.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-LIST-APPLICATIONS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.33" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-list-applications \- list the defined applications and their state
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap list-applications
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIPREFIX\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Lists the defined applications: runtime, artifact, autoscaler bounds, the
identity each runs as, the state it
.I should
be in, and how many instances the manager currently has running.
.PP
.I desiredState
is what
.BR euclid-cli-eap-start-application (1)
and
.BR euclid-cli-eap-stop-application (1)
write;
.I state
and
.I instances
are read back from the manager's own pools. The two differ while an
application is coming up or going down, and stay different if it cannot
start at all - an application whose desiredState is RUNNING with zero
instances is one to look for in the Euclid log.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix " " \fIPREFIX\fR
Only list applications whose ID starts with this.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The list is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
The caller is not an authenticated administrator, or the request could
not be sent.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap list-applications
euclid-cli eap list-applications --prefix inbox
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-get-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-list-modules (1)
